ion or the rule. Indeed, one may ask the following (perhaps optimistic)\nquestion.\nQuestion 6.6.5. What is the probability that the assertion in the bunkbed conjecture for site percolation\nBBC(S1(G)) holds for G ∼G(n, p) the Erd˝os-R´enyi random graph for n large? To what extent does\nthis probability depend on the behaviour of p as a function of n?\nThere are of course many other classes of graph one could consider; graphs of diameter 2 (or simply\nof radius 1), and highly symmetric graphs (e.g. those with transitive automorphism group) would seem\nto be natural candidates for investigation.\nReturning now to the original bunkbed conjecture BBC(E(G)), as was discussed in Section 1.5.1, the\nwork presented here has since been used by Gladkov, Pak and Zimin [63] to construct a counterexample\nto the bunkbed conjecture. This has constituted a rapid reversal of the direction of the previous literature,\nwhich had been almost entirely dedicated to attempts to prove that the bunkbed conjecture true.\nNow that the conjecture is known to be false in general, it is not immediately clear what the next\ndirection for work in the area should be. One question to ask is for which graphs the bunkbed conjecture--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe bunkbed conjecture is not robust to generalisation (https://api.repository.cam.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/3a9595ae-7514-4075-b8e4-721c65d1cee0/content)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Thus it remains to construct directed multigraph *D₇*, and remove the conditioning of model *D₆(D₆,T₆,F₆)* on *T₆* and *F₆*, and the assumption that edges are present in only one bunk.\nL. Hollom                                                                 European Journal of Combinatorics 128 (2025) 104188\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nLeft figure: a grey triangle hyperedge labeled *f* with vertices *u* at the top, *v* at the lower left, and *w* at the lower right.\n